  "kind": "quirk",
  "title": "FACT rejects negative fractions before truncating while FACTDOUBLE admits [-1, 0); both multiply in reverse",
  "statement": "In Excel 16.0 build 20430, FACT(n) checks n >= 0 before truncating toward zero (so FACT(-0.1) is #NUM!) and returns #NUM! for n >= 171, whereas FACTDOUBLE(n) truncates first and admits [-1, 0) as 1 (so FACTDOUBLE(-0.1) = 1 and FACTDOUBLE(-1) = 1, while FACTDOUBLE(-1.1) = #NUM!). For n >= 2, both functions multiply in descending reverse order in native binary64 (n * (n - 1) * ... * 2 for FACT, n * (n - 2) * ... for FACTDOUBLE); forward products miss large values such as FACT(170). All 1,199 FACT and 1,199 FACTDOUBLE full-bank rows match.",
  "status": "measured",
  "basis": "Measured across CalcFunc-Evidence runs/w111-broad-20260929/answers/answers-fact.json.gz and answers-factdouble.json.gz (Excel 16.0 build 20430); 1,199/1,199 FACT and 1,199/1,199 FACTDOUBLE full-bank rows match.",
